+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+The current implementation spreads product quantity checks across `ShoppingCartFacadeImpl`, checkout/order orchestration, and product mapping. Product quantity is stored on the product entity and exposed through the v2 product response, while cart and order lines identify products independently. The change must make those identifiers the basis of every inventory mutation and preserve all-or-nothing behavior at the service transaction boundary.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Centralize stock validation and mutation so every endpoint uses the same matching product and quantity semantics.
+- Make cart mutations and checkout atomic, including multi-item operations.
+- Make cancellation idempotent and prevent invalid transitions from canceled orders.
+- Keep merchant-controlled availability distinct from stock-derived availability.
+- Add endpoint/service regression tests covering success and conflict cases.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Redesign product variants or introduce reservations, backorders, or new persistence tables.
+- Change pricing, payment, shipping, or unrelated order status semantics.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Use the product entity quantity as the inventory source.** This is the quantity already surfaced by the v2 product endpoint, so cart, checkout, cancellation, and presentation cannot drift between separate inventory records. An independent inventory ledger would require migration and reconciliation outside this ticket.
+- **Validate all lines before writing any line.** Cart multi-update and checkout first resolve every product and compare requested quantities, then apply mutations in one transaction. This avoids partial updates when a later line fails; per-line best-effort updates are explicitly unsuitable.
+- **Match mutations by the cart/order line's product identity.** Each decrement and restoration resolves the line's product rather than relying on list position or a shared mutable object, preventing another product from being modified.
+- **Treat cancellation as a state transition with an idempotency guard.** Only a non-canceled to CANCELED transition restores inventory; a repeated CANCELED request does not. A canceled order is terminal for this API.
+- **Compute response purchase flags from merchant availability and quantity.** Merchant unavailability remains authoritative; otherwise zero quantity makes both flags false and positive quantity permits purchase. This keeps the API truthful without altering merchant settings.
+- **Translate stock conflicts at the REST boundary to HTTP 409.** Domain/service code can use the existing exception conventions, while both anonymous and authenticated controllers expose the same conflict contract.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Concurrent carts can race between validation and decrement] → Perform validation and mutation in a transaction with appropriate product row locking or atomic conditional updates, and surface a conflict when the conditional update affects fewer rows than requested.
+- [Existing tests may encode permissive cart behavior] → Update those expectations and add focused tests for unchanged state after conflicts.
+- [Legacy orders may have already been canceled without an inventory marker] → Base idempotency on the persisted prior status and document that only transitions handled by this logic restore stock.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Deploy the service and API changes together. No schema migration is required if order status and product quantity persistence already exist. Roll back by reverting the application release; inventory values changed by a successful checkout or cancellation are business data and require normal operational reconciliation if a rollback follows a partial deployment.
