+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Product inventory is not being treated as the authoritative quantity throughout cart, checkout, order cancellation, and product presentation. This causes incorrect stock decrements, overselling, unrecoverable stock on cancellation, and availability flags that disagree with the quantity shown to shoppers.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Make the product quantity returned by the product API the single stock value used by cart, checkout, and order status transitions.
+- Validate every cart creation and quantity update against current stock, with atomic multi-item updates and HTTP 409 Conflict on insufficient stock.
+- Atomically validate and decrement each product ordered during both authenticated and anonymous checkout; refuse the complete checkout when any line cannot be fulfilled.
+- Restore ordered quantities exactly once when an order is canceled, and reject any later status transition away from CANCELED.
+- Derive shopper-facing `available` and `canBePurchased` from stock while preserving a merchant's explicit unavailable setting.
+- Add regression coverage for per-product decrements, atomic failures, cart limits, cancellation idempotency, status transitions, and product flags.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+
+- `stock-management`: Enforces product stock across cart operations, checkout, cancellation, and product availability.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+<!-- No existing capability specs are present in this checkout. -->
+
+## Impact
+
+Affected Java code includes the shopping-cart facade and v1 cart APIs, checkout/order facades and APIs, order status handling, product quantity/availability mapping, and the corresponding model/repository/service tests. The public REST behavior adds HTTP 409 responses for stock conflicts and status conflicts; no new external dependency is required.
